ng; do NOT use the inline validator path.\n\n====================================================================\nSOURCE-MATERIAL PRIORITY (HARD CONSTRAINT)\n====================================================================\n\nUSE ONLY sources from `corpus/rhetorical_ontology`. No external/web sources. No sources from outside this corpus.\n\nRETRIEVAL PRIORITY (in this order):\n1. `corpus/index/` — consult Bekker reverse-index, BT-structured index, BCAP index FIRST for locus identification.\n2. ChromaDB collections (`metaphysics`, `new_media`, `rhetorical_ontology`) — query for chunked content keyed to the Bekker / SZ / BCAP loci identified in step 1.\n3. Source PDFs in `corpus/rhetorical_ontology/` — fall back ONLY when chunks are missing or insufficient. Cite by Bekker / SZ-H / BCAP-page even when content comes from PDF.\n\nPermitted primary sources for THIS section:\n- Aristotle, *De Insomniis* / *On Dreams* (460b3–11; 460b3–16; 461b3–8; 461b21–462a8) — the feedback mechanism's central textual ground\n- Aristotle, *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 — judgment-affecting definition (cross-reference; established in 1.2)\n- Aristotle, *De Anima* III.3, 427b21–24 — *doxa* produces emotion immediately (cross-reference; established in 1.4)\n- Aristotle, *De Anima* I.4, 408b5–7 — pleasure, pain, thinking as *kinēseis* of the soul (cross-reference; established in 1.2)\n- Aristotle, *Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a14–b2 — *hexis*-formation through habituation; \"we become builders by building\"\n- Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* (GA 18) — pp. 119, 122, 125, 173–176 — *hexis* as \"I am there, come what may\"; *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"; *hexis* as \"how of *pathos*\" (cross-ref); *pathē* as ground of speaking (cross-ref)\n- Heidegger, *Being and Time* — §29 (H.134, H.135, H.136, H.137, H.139–140); §40 (H.184–191) — anxiety as paradigmatic state-of-mind (only if needed for the prior-disclosedness argument); §65 (H.323–331) — primordial temporality of care; §68 sub-section b (H.339–345) — temporality of state-of-mind / moods temporalize \"primarily in having-been\"\n\nPermitted secondary sources for THIS section:\n- None. The argument is wholly developed from the Aristotelian and Heideggerian primary sources above.\n\nNOT PERMITTED for this section:\n- White, Caston, Frede, Nussbaum, O'Gorman, Hawhee, Papachristou, Bowin, Burke, Rickert, Gibson, von Uexküll — these belong to other sections of the chapter (perception, phantasia, action). Do not deploy them in 1.7.\n- Heidegger and Rhetoric (Gross ed.), Gross, Uncomfortable Situations — out of scope for 1.7 even if available; this section is structural-philosophical, not reception-historical.\n\n====================================================================\nMANDATORY OUTPUT STRUCTURE — PRODUCE EXACTLY THIS HIERARCHY\n====================================================================\n\nThe output MUST use exactly ONE heading:\n\n`\\subsection{The Feedback Loop: Synchronic Chain, Diachronic Saturation}` — single polished subsection; approximately 2,300 words of body prose.\n\nDO NOT introduce `\\subsubsection{}` divisions. The section's argument has clear phases (the *De Insomniis* mechanism; the synchronic/diachronic distinction; the two-mechanism analysis; the *hexis*/*Geworfenheit* temporality framing) but these are paragraph groupings, not heading-level divisions. The rest of the chapter's sections (1.3–1.6, 1.8) follow the single-subsection-no-subsubsection convention; 1.7 must match.\n\nThe internal argumentative arc, in order, with approximate word allocations:\n\n(A) **Opening hinge from 1.6** (~150 words) — picks up from §1.6's closing claim that emotion is \"the soul's kinetic alignment toward a world disclosed as mattering in a particular way.\" States that the linear chain $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ established in §1.6 cannot fully capture a structural feature entailed by the *Rhetoric*'s judgment-affecting definition: emotion, once produced, alters the conditions under which subsequent *doxai* are formed. Names this as the section's task.\n\n(B) **The *De Insomniis* mechanism** (~600 words) — develops the feedback phenomenon. Quotes *De Insomniis* 460b3–11 in body (not footnote) for its load-bearing analytic content; uses 460b3–16 for the controlling-sense apparatus. Develops the self-reinforcing structure: *doxa* produces emotion; emotion impairs the controlling sense; subsequent *phantasmata* pass into *doxa* unopposed; new *doxai* produce further emotions. Names the four illustrative phenomena Aristotle's text supports: the escalation of anger through sustained rumination on the perceived slight; the intensification of fear through repeated imagining of the anticipated evil; the susceptibility of the dreamer, the ill, and the passionately agitated to phantasmatic deception (citing *De Insomniis* 460b3–16; 461b3–8); the rhetor's strategy in the *Rhetoric* of first establishing emotional attunement to weaken the audience's corrective faculties. Closes with the qualification that the loop is not a defect of the system but an intrinsic feature of the relationship between emotion and *doxa* in rational animals.\n\n(C) **The interpretive-reconstruction caveat** (~150 words) — folded into a footnote attached to the close of phase (B). Acknowledges that Aristotle does not state the feedback loop in these terms: *DA* III.3, 427b21–24 establishes the *doxa*-emotion immediacy; *De Insomniis* 460b3–16 establishes the controlling-faculty impairment; *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 establishes the judgment-affecting structure. That these passages describe aspects of a single recursive mechanism is a plausible interpretive synthesis, not a doctrinally-explicit Aristotelian claim. The synthesis is, however, philosophically productive, and the linear $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ notation cannot fully represent it.\n\n(D) **The synchronic/diachronic distinction** (~500 words) — the section's analytic core. Two registers are at work and must be distinguished. The synchronic register concerns the structural transitions at any single moment of *pathos*-actualization: basic valence grounds *doxa*; *doxa* concretizes the higher-order *pathē*; the higher-order *pathē* mobilize action. The diachronic register concerns how prior actualizations modulate subsequent actualizations: a *pathos*-actualization at $T_1$ persists as dispositional ground for the *pathos*-actualization at $T_2$. The *De Insomniis* phenomenon belongs to the diachronic register, not the synchronic register. The chain remains structurally directional from $A_0$ to $A_4$ within any single actualization; the \"loop\" is the temporal persistence of prior actualizations as ground for subsequent actualizations, not a third synchronic transition within a given chain-pass. Specify the two distinct mechanisms within the diachronic register: (1) *Stimmung*-saturation, modulating the *input* to *doxa* — a sustained higher-order *pathos* persists as a tonal coloring that biases subsequent perception; the angry person sees enemies in shadows because the anger-*Stimmung* re-saturates the perceptual encounter. (2) Corrective-faculty impairment (the mechanism Aristotle specifies in *De Insomniis*), modulating the *gating* of *doxa* — emotional excitement disables the controlling sense, so that *phantasmata* that would normally be intercepted pass into *doxa* unopposed. Both are diachronic modulations of synchronic transitions, not new synchronic transitions. Mechanism (1) modulates basic valence at $A_0$ as a function of prior actualizations; mechanism (2) modulates the *doxa*-gate's content-conditionality.\n\n(E) **The *hexis*/*Geworfenheit* temporality framing** (~700 words) — develops the closing argument that the diachronic register is *pathos*-grade thrownness. Open with the Aristotelian articulation: *hexis* as \"settled disposition,\" formed through repeated actualization. Quote *NE* II.1, 1103a14–b2 (\"moral excellence comes about as a result of habit\"; \"we become builders by building, lyre-players by playing the lyre, just by doing just acts, temperate by doing temperate acts, brave by doing brave acts\"). Argue that each actualization of a *pathos*-chain leaves a residual modification of the dispositional ground; over time, repeated actualizations consolidate into *hexeis* that condition subsequent chain-actualizations. *Hexis* is not a third stage of the chain but the temporal sedimentation of repeated chain-actualizations into a dispositional ground. Develop the Heideggerian articulation: *Being and Time* §29 establishes that state-of-mind has three essential characteristics (it discloses Dasein in its thrownness; it has already disclosed Being-in-the-world as a whole; it discloses worldhood in the mode of circumspective concern), and that thrownness names the diachronic persistence of prior dispositional states (Dasein is always already in a *Stimmung* that conditions its disclosure; the *Stimmung* carries forward from prior actualizations). Cite *BT* §68b on moods temporalizing \"primarily in having-been\" (SZ §68, H.340; Eng. 390): *Befindlichkeit*'s having-already-found-itself-disposed is not a past stored in memory but a *Gewesenheit* (having-been) that *currently* conditions how the world's entities can come close. Argue that GA 18's *hexis*-analysis is the lectures-prefiguration of this *Geworfenheit*-temporality — quote BCAP p. 122 on the fourth aspect of being-there derived from *hexis* (\"Being-there must, *for itself*, take the opportunity to cultivate this being-composed as a possibility\"; *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"). Close the phase with the structural thesis: the feedback is not a structurally distinct third direction but the *thrownness-character* of *pathos* — the temporal persistence of prior actualizations as mood-ground for subsequent encounters. The Aristotelian *hexis* and the Heideggerian *Geworfenheit* are two articulations of one phenomenon — prior dispositional actualization persists as the ground from which present disclosure takes its character — at different ontological registers (Aristotle's at the perception-and-affection level within the soul-structure; Heidegger's at the level of Dasein's fundamental disclosedness).\n\n(F) **Closing transition to 1.8** (~100 words) — restate the section's conclusion: the actualization chain is synchronically directional but diachronically saturated. The *pathos*-chain runs $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ within any single moment but each moment's $A_0$ is itself the having-been-shaped of prior runs. Transition forward: with the chain's synchronic directionality and diachronic temporality both specified, the analysis can now turn (in §1.8) to the question of emotion's specific causal function within the chain — what work emotion does at $M_3 \\rightarrow M_4$ that *orexis* alone could not.\n\n====================================================================\nPER-PHASE REQUIREMENTS — DETAILED INSTRUCTIONS\n====================================================================\n\n**Phase A — Opening Hinge (~150 words).**\n\nPRESERVE the substance of source line 3's first sentence (\"However, this structure alone does not fully encompass the role emotion plays in animal movement as Aristotle presents it\"). Re-cast in the polished register so it functions as a transition from §1.6's closing image. Cross-reference the *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 judgment-affecting definition (no need to re-quote; it was quoted in §1.2 and is in working memory for the chapter).\n\nDO NOT introduce *De Insomniis* in this phase. Save the quotation for Phase B.\n\nDO NOT re-establish the linear $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ chain. The chain is established in §1.6; reference it as \"the linear chain established in the prior section\" or \"the actualization chain $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$\" without re-development.\n\n**Phase B — The *De Insomniis* Mechanism (~600 words).**\n\nCONSOLIDATE source lines 3, 5, and 7 into a single non-redundant development. The three are substantially duplicate; the polished version draws from the cleanest formulations of each:\n\n- The opening framing uses source line 5's cleaner prose (\"The *Rhetoric*'s definition of emotion as judgment-affecting [*Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22] entails a structural feature that the linear chain cannot fully capture\").\n- The *De Insomniis* 460b3–11 quotation goes in the BODY (not a footnote), per source line 5's placement. The full quotation: \"We are easily deceived respecting the operations of sense-perception when we are excited by emotions, and different persons according to their different emotions; for example, the coward when excited by fear, the amorous person by the object of his desire; so that, with but little resemblance to go upon, the former thinks he sees his foes approaching, the latter, that he sees the object of his desire; and the more deeply one is under the influence of the emotion, the less similarity is required to give rise to these impressions\" (*De Insomniis* 460b3–11).\n- The longer *De Insomniis* 460b3–20 passage in source line 3's footnote (containing the second half on the controlling sense and the sun's diameter) goes into a SUPPORTING FOOTNOTE attached to the body sentence introducing the controlling-sense impairment. Quote the relevant clauses: \"The cause of these occurrences is that the faculty in virtue of which the controlling sense judges is not identical with that in virtue of which images come before the mind\" (*De Insomniis* 460b16–18). The sun-as-foot-in-diameter example may be retained as the closing line of the footnote.\n- The self-reinforcing-loop sentence (\"An initial *doxa* produces an initial emotion; the emotion impairs the corrective faculty; subsequent *phantasmata* pass through the gate into *doxa* unopposed; the new *doxai* produce further emotions\") preserves source line 7's cleaner version. Append the source line 7 closing about the process continuing \"until some external intervention, some exhaustion of the somatic preparation, or some recovery of the corrective faculty breaks the recursion.\"\n- The four illustrative phenomena (rumination/anger; imagining/fear; dreamer-ill-passionate susceptibility; rhetor's audience attunement) consolidate source lines 3 and 7. Use source line 7's cleaner version. Cite *De Insomniis* 460b3–16 and 461b3–8 at the dreamer-ill-passionate phrase.\n- The closing claim — \"The loop is not a defect of the psychological system but an intrinsic feature of the relationship between emotion and *doxa* in rational animals\" — preserves source line 7 verbatim.\n\nRESOLVE the empty `\\hl{(CITE)}\\footnote{}` stub at source line 3 (after \"the susceptibility of the dream, the ill, and the passionately agitated to phantasmatic deception\"). The proper citation is *De Insomniis* 461b3–8 — already used in source line 7. The empty footnote should be DROPPED entirely; the citation goes inline at the end of the sentence enumerating the four phenomena.\n\nThe *Rhetoric* \"first establishing an emotional attunement\" sentence about the rhetor's strategy: PRESERVE source line 7. Cross-reference is to the *Rhetoric*'s general practical framework, not a single Bekker locus; no specific Bekker is required, but `(\\textit{Rhetoric} II, passim)` is acceptable if a citation is desired.\n\n**Phase C — Interpretive-Reconstruction Caveat (~150 words, in a footnote).**\n\nSource line 9 contains the methodological qualification. ABSORB this into a SINGLE FOOTNOTE attached to the closing sentence of Phase B (\"The loop is not a defect of the psychological system but an intrinsic feature of the relationship between emotion and *doxa* in rational animals\"). The footnote should run approximately 5–7 sentences:\n\n- Acknowledge that the feedback loop, as developed here, is an interpretive reconstruction rather than a doctrine Aristotle states in these terms.\n- Specify the three loci that the synthesis brings together: *DA* III.3, 427b21–24 (*doxa* produces emotion immediately); *De Insomniis* 460b3–16 (emotional states permit *phantasmata* to be taken as true when the controlling faculty is impaired); *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 (emotions modify *kriseis*).\n- State that each passage addresses, strictly speaking, a different phenomenon, and that their unification under a single recursive mechanism is a plausible but not indisputable interpretive claim.\n- Close with the philosophical-productivity claim: the synthesis is productive because the linear $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ notation cannot fully represent the feedback structure; a complete topological representation would require a return path from the higher-order *pathos* node back to the gatekeeping condition modulating subsequent *doxa*-formation.\n\nDO NOT include this material in the body. It belongs in the footnote.\n\n**Phase D — Synchronic/Diachronic Distinction and Two-Mechanism Analysis (~500 words).**\n\nDEVELOP the analytic core from source lines 11, 33, 35, 37, 39. The source has substantial duplication across these lines (the synchronic/diachronic distinction is stated three times; the two-mechanism analysis twice; the D1/D2/D3 notation appears in two slightly different formulations). The polished version states each component once, in the cleanest form.\n\nThe argument's structure:\n\n- (i) Two registers: synchronic (structural transitions within a single *pathos*-actualization) vs. diachronic (modulation of subsequent actualizations by prior ones). State the synchronic register's components: $A_0 \\rightarrow A_1$ basic valence grounds *doxa*; $A_2 \\rightarrow A_3$ *doxa* concretizes higher-order *pathē*; $A_3 \\rightarrow A_4$ higher-order *pathē* mobilize action via somatic preparation. State the diachronic register's components: prior actualizations of the chain at $T_1$ modulate the basic valence and *doxa*-gate-conditions at $T_2$.\n- (ii) The *De Insomniis* phenomenon belongs to the diachronic register, not the synchronic register. The chain remains structurally directional within any single actualization; the \"loop\" is the temporal persistence of prior actualizations as ground for subsequent actualizations.\n- (iii) Two distinct mechanisms operate within the diachronic register:\n  - **Mechanism (1) — Stimmung-saturation.** A sustained higher-order *pathos* persists as a tonal coloring that biases subsequent perception. Modulates the *input* to *doxa* (the basic valence at $A_0$). Concrete illustration: the angry person sees enemies in shadows because the anger-*Stimmung* re-saturates the perceptual encounter.\n  - **Mechanism (2) — Corrective-faculty impairment.** Emotional excitement disables the controlling sense, so that *phantasmata* that would normally be intercepted pass into *doxa* unopposed. Modulates the *gating* of *doxa*. This is the mechanism Aristotle explicitly specifies in *De Insomniis*; mechanism (1) is the structural complement that the *Stimmung*-saturation language brings into focus.\n  - The two are commonly conflated under \"feedback loop\" but operate at different points in the chain: (1) at the basic-valence input; (2) at the corrective-gate.\n- (iv) Both mechanisms are diachronic modulations of synchronic transitions, not new synchronic transitions. Stated formally: mechanism (1) specifies that the basic valence at $A_0$ is itself a function of prior actualizations (the basic valence brought to a perceptual encounter is shaped by prior dispositional history); mechanism (2) specifies that the *doxa*-gate's content-conditionality is itself modulated by prior actualizations (emotional excitement disables the discriminative filter, so the gate fires more readily under sustained *pathē*).\n\nDO NOT use the D1/D2/D3 notation from source lines 31–35. This notation is internal scaffolding; the polished prose should refer to \"the synchronic transitions\" and \"the apparent feedback direction\" or \"what appears to be a third structural transition\" rather than D1, D2, D3 letters. The notation may be retained internally for the writer's clarity but must not appear in the output.\n\nDO NOT re-introduce or re-quote *De Insomniis* in this phase. It was quoted and developed in Phase B; here it is referenced.\n\n**Phase E — *Hexis*/*Geworfenheit* Temporality (~700 words).**\n\nDEVELOP from source lines 13, 15, 17, 19, 21, 23, 25, 27, 29, 41, 43. The source has substantial duplication and disorganization across these lines. The polished version develops the argument in three movements:\n\n- (i) **The Aristotelian articulation: *hexis*** (~250 words). Open with *NE* II.1, 1103a14–b2. Quote: \"moral excellence comes about as a result of habit\" (1103a16–17), and the canonical formulation: \"For the things we have to learn before we can do, we learn by doing, e.g. men become builders by building and lyre-players by playing the lyre; so too we become just by doing just acts, temperate by doing temperate acts, brave by doing brave acts\" (*NE* II.1, 1103a31–b2). Argue that each actualization of a *pathos*-chain leaves a residual modification of the dispositional ground; over time, repeated actualizations consolidate into *hexeis* that condition subsequent chain-actualizations. *Hexis* is not a third stage of the chain but the temporal sedimentation of repeated chain-actualizations into a dispositional ground. Cross-reference (briefly) the *hexis*-as-how-of-*pathos* claim already established in §1.2's footnote 1 (BCAP p. 125) — do not re-quote, just refer.\n\n- (ii) **The Heideggerian articulation: *Befindlichkeit* and *Geworfenheit*** (~300 words). Open with the systematic formulation from *BT* §29. Develop the three essential characteristics of state-of-mind: (1) it discloses Dasein in its thrownness; (2) it has already disclosed Being-in-the-world as a whole, making it possible first of all to direct oneself toward something; (3) it discloses the worldhood of the world in the mode of circumspective concern (cite SZ §29, H.137; Eng. 176). State that thrownness names the diachronic persistence of prior dispositional states: Dasein is always already in a *Stimmung* that conditions its disclosure; the *Stimmung* carries forward from prior actualizations. Quote selectively from BT 174/175 the thrownness passage: \"this characteristic of Dasein's Being—this 'that it is'—is veiled in its 'whence' and 'whither', yet disclosed in itself all the more unveiledly; we call it the 'thrownness' [*Geworfenheit*] of this entity into its 'there'\" (SZ §29, H.135; Eng. 174). Develop the temporality claim from *BT* §68b: \"Understanding is grounded primarily in the future; one's *state-of-mind*, however, temporalizes itself *primarily* in *having been*. Moods temporalize themselves—that is, their specific ecstasis belongs to a future and a Present in such a way, indeed, that these equiprimordial ecstases are modified by having-been\" (SZ §68, H.340; Eng. 390). Argue that *Befindlichkeit*'s having-already-found-itself-disposed is not a past stored in memory but a *Gewesenheit* (having-been) that *currently* conditions how the world's entities can come close.\n\n- (iii) **The convergence: GA 18 *hexis* prefigures *BT* *Geworfenheit*** (~150 words). Cite BCAP p. 122 (the four basic aspects of being-there derived from *hexis*; the fourth aspect: \"Being-there must, *for itself*, take the opportunity to cultivate this being-composed as a possibility\"; *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"). Argue that the lectures' fourth aspect of being-there derived from *hexis* is the lectures' formulation of the diachronic dispositional persistence that *Geworfenheit* will name in *Being and Time*. Close with the structural thesis: the feedback is not a structurally distinct third direction but the thrownness-character of *pathos* — the temporal persistence of prior actualizations as mood-ground for subsequent encounters. The Aristotelian *hexis* and the Heideggerian *Geworfenheit* are two articulations